A||N||9||Jeffrx7||86.5 gt runs for only 2 seconds...why?||||1||
Z||000000||Jeffrx7||10-03-2001||11:06 PM||||I just picked up a gt for my winter car, the only problem is, it doesn't quite run...really...  It will fire up right away, but then stops running abruptly after about 2 seconds. crank it again, same thing. over and over.  The person I bought it from said that it used to just do it every once in a while, but other times it would be fine, now it only does that.  He replaced a whole bunch of sensors on the engine, plus a new fuel pump and filter. (holds fuel pressure).  Anyone have any idea what it sounds like the prob is?  Anything I can test to narrow it down?  possibly a bad ecm? Also the plugs get somewhat fuel soaked after trying to start it a few times.  Any help would be greatly appreciated! Thanks<P>~Jeff||||reg||1
Z||000001||yo_sweet||10-03-2001||11:12 PM||||do you hear your fuel pump when you start?,try this open up your rubber whose that runs to the injectors and sqirt thiners or gasoline in your injectors then see how long the car will run,this happend to me before i squirted the fuel in the injectors and it started no problem jus have to let it run for a couple of minites.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||10-04-2001||05:53 AM||||Sounds like only the cold start injector is firing. Possible bad old pressure sending unit or bad connection at EMC.<BR>One car I had with the same symptoms had mouse damage to a couple of wires coming off the EMC, Due to setting up for a long period. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jeffrx7||10-04-2001||09:14 AM||||Wolf, I'm curious why you believe it could be the oil pressure sending unit.  The reason for this is because i did find a plug disconnected from the sending unit, and the previous owner said that it was disconnected because the gauge never worked? It is some 4 prong plug. could that possibly cause a weird prob like this? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||10-04-2001||10:05 AM||||Are you getting any "Error" codes? The oil pressure sending unit is connected in series with the fuel pump relay as a precaution. If your fuel pressure drops, the relay cuts out and the car will die. Your problem could be either the oil pressure sending unit or the fuel pump relay or both.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Archie||10-04-2001||10:08 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jeffrx7:<BR><B>Wolf, I'm curious why you believe it could be the oil pressure sending unit.  The reason for this is because i did find a plug disconnected from the sending unit, and the previous owner said that it was disconnected because the gauge never worked? It is some 4 prong plug. could that possibly cause a weird prob like this? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well this IS your problem.<P>I don't work on stock Fieros but when I saw the title of this thread I knew it would be an easy one to solve.<P>Your car WILL NOT RUN with that plug un-plugged. You'll notice 3 wires running into that plug. The middle wire is the wire that operates the gage. The other 2 wires complete a circuit that, when activated by the Oil Pressure sending unit, runs the fuel pump when there is oil pressure. If there is no oil pressure, it won't keep running.<P>You can test this by running a jumper wire between the 2 terminals and you will be able to run the car. BTW, this jumper wire will run the Fuel Pump ALL THE TIME even when the car is shut off. So this is not a cure, only a test.<P>We already know that your Fuel Pump is working, otherwise you wouldn't be able to run the car AT ALL & you would have no Fuel pressure.<P>The cure....<P>Either a bad oil pressure sender or just the fact that the idiot un-plugged the sensor is what is keeping this thing from working.<P>Try this..... plug the wire back in (with the key off) and if you hear the fuel pump running them you have a bad sending unit. If it doesn't run the fuel pump when you plug it in AND when you start the car it runs ok then the problem was just the fact that it was un-plugged.<P>The reason the car will run for a couple of seconds when you first start it is because the act of turning the key on runs the fuel pump for a couple of seconds to prime the system. Positive Oil pressure is what runs the pump after the engine has started. After running a few seconds if there is no input that tells the ECM that there is oil pressure (either from the wire being unplugged or from a bad sending unit) then the car shuts down because the fuel pump is not running.<P>AS I said, I don't work on stock Fieros BUT when I do V-8 conversions on Fieros, I use this same circuitry so I've got a lot of experience in this area.<P>Sure it could be in other areas of the system like the ECM But I HIGHLY DOUBT IT.<P>Archie <p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||10-04-2001||09:44 PM||||I'm not doubting anyone here, but I thought the oil press switch and the fuel pump relay could both be energized at the same time. See the diagram How does the oil pressure switch kick out the fuel pump relay? According to the diagram, the lead from the oil pressure switch goes directly to the fuel pump, whether the fuel pump relay is energized or not. (as long as there is adequate oil press). Thought the ecm would keep the fuel pump relay closed as long as it was getting ign pulses? Am I reading the diagram wrong?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fuelinj_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jeffrx7||10-05-2001||12:13 AM||||The way I understand it maryjane, you are correct.  The way my Chilton manual explains it, the oil pressure switch is in place in the case that the fuel pump relay would fail. once the oil pressure reaches 4psi that switch can then be used to bypass the the fuel pump relay if needed.  I was just outside and tried jumping the two terminals in that disconnected plug from that switch.  I heard the fuel pump humm, then tried starting and it went on the third try and ran fine.  This leads me to believe that I have a bad fuel pump relay, since this "safety switch" had been unplugged by the previous owner, and once the terminals were jumped, it cured it.  I ran out of light to work on it(other cars in garage...) so Im going to test the relay tomorrow morning, and that combined with reconnecting the switch will with any luck solve my problems!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fallman||10-05-2001||06:52 PM||||Okay guys i dont now what the chilton manual says, (well actually i do i have it) but i had the same problem, i unhooked the oil thing amagig and crossed those too point walla the pump works so i go an buy both the oil pressure sensor and the relay. i installed the relay first cause it was cheaper then tried to start the car and it ran just fine. needless to say i will be returning the $38 oil pressure sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wolfhound||10-05-2001||07:42 PM||||The ECM only opperates the Fuel pump for initial starting. I think for 3 seconds?<BR>then the pressure guage curcuit takes over. This keep you from having to build oil pressure to crank.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/starun.JPG"> <BR>Coy||||reg||1
